      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat: add metadata-only message audit events",
        "body": "Related: #103605\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nOpenClaw's existing structured audit stream records agent runs and tool actions, but it does not provide a bounded message-lifecycle record. Operators therefore have to infer whether accepted inbound messages reached core dispatch or whether durable outbound messages reached a terminal delivery state by parsing general-purpose logs.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis adds an additive, versioned `audit.activity.list` surface while preserving the existing `audit.list` contract. It stores metadata-only inbound and outbound terminal events in the shared SQLite audit store, adds the opt-in `audit.messages: off | direct | all` policy (default `off`), and correlates platform identifiers with installation-local, domain-separated HMAC references rather than raw identifiers.\n\nInbound events are recorded at accepted core dispatch; outbound events are recorded at the shared durable-delivery terminal boundary, including recovery and dead-letter outcomes. The implementation remains bounded and best-effort rather than claiming lossless compliance-ledger semantics. OpenTelemetry and Prometheus exporters are intentionally unchanged because OpenClaw already ships first-party exporters; this change fills the missing audit-lifecycle layer.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOperators can opt into direct-message or all-message audit metadata and query a closed V1 schema through the CLI/gateway without storing message bodies, attachments, tool arguments/results, or raw error text. Pseudonymous references are stable within an installation, but are not anonymization because the HMAC key lives in the same database.\n\nCoverage is deliberately explicit: pre-admission inbound paths and plugin/direct-send outbound paths that bypass shared durable delivery are not recorded. Absence of an activity row is therefore not proof that no message existed.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Focused AWS Crabbox matrix `run_a7a18ef39200`: 973 tests passed across protocol, gateway, audit/storage, CLI, auto-reply, agents, and outbound delivery/recovery.\n- Full changed-surface gate `run_5ae413c847e1`: typecheck, lint, database/sidecar guards, generated-surface checks, and runtime import-cycle checks passed.\n- Production build `run_5c663c8a1f04`: passed; public plugin SDK declaration graph 5,027,660 / 5,050,000 bytes.\n- Fresh GPT-5.5 autoreview: no actionable findings; patch judged correct (0.82 confidence).\n- Final rebase onto current `origin/main` was conflict-free; intervening upstream commits touched no PR files.\n\nAI-assisted implementation and review.\n",
